+# T-3952 Track partners, turns and parity (track rework stage 3a2)
+
+Agent trackparity, 2026-11-21, tree `.tw-trees/trackparity` (branch tw/trackparity from tw/track 5af6e0d7), Vite 6174.
+Design: notes/T-3444-design/01-model.md "Node" (4, turn) and "Bundle" (partner), 05-sim.md sections 3 and 6,
+10-build-plan.md row 3a2. Advisor consulted twice. Before building it set: partners derived in the compile (no
+`EdgeData` field), turn moves in a list of their own beside `moves`, as wide as today's rule rather than the
+plan's "to its partner", and `turns_back` kept as the side test with a separate table check for permission, so
+parity holds by construction; plus a fresh fingerprint and rides baseline first, and tests that drop turns. Before
+handing back it asked for the smoke result, a check that `broken` is recomputed on load, the Vienna cause worded as
+unverified, and repeat timings for NA.
+
+## What changed
+
+- **Partners** (`compile.rs`, `Track::partner`): derived in `compile_piece` from the piece. On double track each track
+  is the other's partner, a two-way single its own, a one-way single has none. Partners never cross pieces, so drawn
+  quad's two pairs (two pieces in one bundle, T-3949) keep their own. `layout` returns each slot's partner (doubles
+  side by side pair 0-1, 2-3; an odd last two-way track its own), so a piece of more tracks gets them too when 3b
+  makes one.
+- **Turn moves** (`NodeTracks::turns`, `turn_to` by piece end as `to` is for moves), compiled in `compile_node`, kept
+  apart from `moves` so crossing tests, the badge, `node_unusable` and the search's through moves never see them:
+  at a station node, from every track end bringing trains in to every track end taking them out on the same side;
+  at a bare end (`turn_end`, no station), from each track end to its partner's on the same piece end. Nowhere else.
+- **What reads them**: `check_route` accepts a turn only by `turn_ok` (the node's table), in place of the side test;
+  the search's turn at a track end mid-route (`find_path_side`'s back step) takes `turn_to` at the arriving end. The
+  search's turn at its first stop stays the side test: `arrived` is a side, not a track, and at a stop the turn moves
+  run from every arriving end on a side to every leaving end there, so the two agree for any train that really
+  arrived. `turns_back` (the side test) stays as the classifier capacity, service, perdir and the loop rule call on
+  runs and candidate paths. Capacity's turning and platform keys stay node keyed until 4a.
+- Tests (`wasm/lanes_tests.rs`): `t3952_partners` (double, two-way and one-way single, drawn quad);
+  `t3952_turns_read_the_table` (the table at bare ends, junctions and a station; a line turning at a station is
+  broken once that station's turns are dropped, `drop_turns`; a route that can only turn at a bare end is no longer
+  found once that end's are).
+- Docs: track-and-service.md "Tracks" (partner, turn moves), code-map.md (`compile.rs`). No player text, no app
+  change, no save change.
+
+## Departures from the plan
+
+- **Partners are derived, not stored.** Row 3a2 says "partners stored (the loader pairs each old double)". There is no
+  stored track record before 3b's TWT7, and today a piece's track count and way fix its partner, so a field on
+  `EdgeData` would be a second copy that splits, undo and the save must carry, with nothing able to set it otherwise.
+  The compile pairs each double on every compile, which for old saves is the loader's pairing. 3b stores the partner
+  with the track record (07-saves) and the converter writes these pairs.
+- **Turn moves are wider than 05-sim section 3's "to its partner".** At a station a turn may leave on any track on
+  its side, not only the partner: today a line may reverse at a stop onto another branch on the same side
+  (`turns_back` plus `check_route`, the search's first-stop turn), and keeping that is what parity needs ("nothing
+  that works today should stop working"). At a bare end a turn is to the partner only, which is exactly what the
+  search allowed (`dir_ok` of the reverse entry). One narrowing: `check_route` used to accept a turn at a track end
+  onto another piece on the same side (a node with one built end and blueprint beside it on the same side); the
+  search never routed such a turn, so no line has one unless placed by hand, and none of the gate saves do.
+
+## Checks
+
+- `agent_cargo.py test` on the final source: both suites pass, 740 + 961 (two new tests).
+- `lanes_parity` fingerprints, base (5af6e0d7, rebuilt here and checked identical to 3a1's `a2` outputs) against the
+  final source, both on the agent profile: **byte identical** on `game`, `game-left`, `mirror-right`, `mirror-left`,
+  Amsterdam test, Vienna, real NYC and North America 383 (every path and return path, reroutes, 600 station pairs,
+  badges, junction inspectors, guard pairs, every capacity resource with users and solved figures, lines' figures).
+  Gate A (real NYC) and gate B (the small games, Amsterdam test, plus Vienna and NA) on paths and capacity.
+  - A first version also read the turn moves at the search's first stop; Vienna's 600 pairs then differed in 12/600
+    (a route found before, none after). Likely cause, not checked node by node: the fingerprint asks `find_path`
+    with an arrival side picked by index, so some pairs start at a stop "arrived" on a side where no track brings
+    trains in (one-way tram track), and the table has no turn from there. The start went back to the side test
+    rather than leave a difference; 3c should confirm the cause before making the first-stop turn read the table.
+- Native timings, base / after (ms), one run each: load NYC 102 / 100, Amsterdam 20 / 20, Vienna 422 / 426, NA 309 /
+  302; 600 station pairs NYC 1.2 / 1.3, Vienna 68 / 73; NA's 600 pairs three runs each, 5.0-5.7 / 5.3-6.3.
+- Rides, in the browser (play.mjs on Vite 6174, 4 cores, the same method both times), the real save: base
+  12,886,431 rail trips a day, 109 lines (master's figure); after, on the final WASM, 12,886,431, 109 lines.
+- `agent_cargo.py wasm` and `npm run check` clean on the final source. Smoke (`SMOKE_PORT=6174 TW_CORES_WANT=4`) on
+  the final state 30 of 30, 207 s; its real-save demand final reads 13,252,828 rail trips a day, 109 lines, as in
+  3a1's smoke (smoke's run differs from play.mjs's; not chased).
+- `line_broken` is recomputed by `check_line` in the derive (derive.rs), so the fingerprints' `broken` column is
+  this build's check, not the save's flag: no gate line is broken by the narrowed turn rule.
+
+Scratch (this session's scratchpad, `trackparity/`): `fp.py`, the four `.bin` cuts (copied from 3a1's), both
+`lanes_parity` builds, `fp/base-*` and `fp/after2-*`.
+
+## For landing (orchestrator)
+
+- DECISIONS line: 2026-11-21 (agent trackparity, T-3952, on tw/track, advisor consulted): each compiled track has a
+  partner (double track's other track, a two-way single itself, a one-way single none, never across pieces, so
+  quad's pairs keep their own), derived in the compile until 3b stores it with the track record; and each node has
+  turn moves apart from its moves: at a station from every arriving track to every leaving track on the same side
+  (a line may still reverse at a stop onto another branch), at a bare end from each track to its partner. Line
+  checks and the search's turn at a track end read them. Fingerprints byte identical on gates A and B.
+  notes/T-3952.md
+- Nothing staged; `notes/T-3952.md` is new, unstaged.
+- No player text changed; no changelog item.
+
+## Left for later stages
+
+- 3b: store `partner` on the track record; the converter writes the pairs the compile derives here.
+- 3c: the return run by partner lookup reads `Track::partner`; the first-stop turn can read the table once paths
+  name tracks (the arrival is then a track, not a side).
+- 8: turn moves on double track give way to real turnback crossovers; the station rule's "any track on its side"
+  is where the reverse-onto-another-branch case needs deciding then.
