+phases with parity after each, the tag bit, the tie-break, track-id arrays, rekeying after an edit, the slot rule at
+plain joints and the station question (all below).
+
+## What changed
+
+- **Packed entries** (`net.rs`): a path entry is `TAG | piece << 5 | key << 1 | dir` (`net::entry`), with
+  `path_edge`, `path_dir`, `path_key`, `with_piece`. The tag (bit 31) is asserted in tests, so a port code
+  (`edge << 1 | end`) used as an entry, or an entry decoded with `>> 1`, fails loudly. `app_entry` / `app_path`
+  give `edge << 1 | dir`, what the app and the fingerprints read: `line_path`, `line_run_path`, `line_back_path`,
+  the guard's junction pairs (`guard_conflicts`, sorted in app order first), related lines' routes and the
+  fingerprint's paths, reroutes, pairs and runs. Every raw `>> 1`, `<< 1 |` and `^ 1` site was sorted into port code
+  or entry (about 60 entry sites); `p ^ 1` became `partner_entry` or `reverse_entry` (the partner, else the first
+  track running the other way).
+- **(piece, dir) to entry** in one place, `Network::entry_for` (the first track in id order running that way): old
+  bodies and TWT4-6 (`path_in`), `realsave.rs`, test paths. Spans read by `span_entry`, written by `span_of`
+  (slot of the key).
+- **Routing on track ends** (`compile.rs`, `net/paths.rs`): `NodeTracks::to` and `turn_to` (port masks) are gone;
+  `moves_from(p)` / `turns_from(p)` give the entries a train arriving on `p`'s track end may take, `move_ok` /
+  `turn_ok` test an exact entry, `port_moves` is the piece-level question (`serves_way`). `find_path_side` and
+  `loop_back` run one `Search` (states by track id and direction, its tables kept between searches; ties pop in app-entry order,
+  then by entry, so equal-time routes come out as before). A rejoin in `loop_back` is an exact move.
+- **Records hold 1-16 tracks of any ways** (`tracks.rs`): `valid` takes 1-16 tracks, keys under 16 and unique, ways
+  0-3, partners that name their track back (or itself); left/right joins stay single track's. `Tracks::new(n)` lays
+  `default_way`'s A B A B from the right with an odd leftmost track two-way. `repair` (run by every `set_edge`):
+  a bundle's ways are all 0 (by the running side) when they are all its defaults, else all its own; partners by
+  01's rule (two-way its own, opposite pairs kept, same-way pairs broken, then the bracket match in slot order).
+  The compile lays each slot's way from its record (`slot_ways`, `layout`), ids a to b first then from the right as
+  before, partners from the records.
+- **Slot i carries on as slot i** (`compile_node`): at a node of two piece ends on opposite sides, same track count,
+  where some way has more than one track, a move joins only the same slot (reversed where one piece runs the other
+  way). Today's pieces never meet the condition.
+- **Stale keys** (`world.rs`): an edit that lays a piece's tracks again (single doubled, a double made single) is
+  followed in the same edit by `rekey_ops` (entries naming no track that runs their way take `entry_for`'s), so undo
+  is exact; the running-side flip rekeys after its reload. Kept returns are left to `back_ops` as before.
+- **Stations**: refused where a piece at the station runs two tracks one way (`check_node`, `Issue::Tracks`):
+  `stop_point` and capacity's platform keys go by side, so two such tracks would share one platform silently.
+- **Save** (no layout change, body level 4 stays): each bundle's tracks are written as the records say
+  (`file_tracks`: ways, partner slots) and read for any count 1-16, any ways and pairs (`tracks_in`); a way of 0 on
+  a bundle, a partner past the tracks, or partners the rule would change count as unread. Paths read any slot the
+  piece has; a slot past it counts as unread.
+- Docs: track-and-service.md "Tracks", architecture.md section 11, code-map.md (`compile.rs`).
