+  line's new end), `t4036_rerouted_to_anchor_as_split` (a piece under a line ending at the station deleted with a way
+  round by `delete_edge`, which leaves the lines over it broken on both twins; each then routed again by `set_stops`,
+  so the reroute tested is the stop list's, not the deletion's own),
+  `t4036_reads_of_a_line_ending_at_anchor_as_split` (the app's per-line reads on a line ending and one starting at the
+  station: run, back and way-out stops, path tracks, passed stations, stop tiers, reversals, stop ways, end turns,
+  loop kind and `train_at` over 12 minutes both runs, equal to the split's, trains to the micrometre on straight track;
+  the path exports, `lines_info` and `nodes_info` read for no failure), `t4036_loop_tools_and_related_at_anchor_as_split`
+  (a line ending at the station turned round a loop past it by `turn_end`; `line_related` for a line stopping there
+  equal to the split's; fails on the old `route_of`), `t4036_picks_at_anchors_as_split` (4a2's one-track pair as two
+  anchors: one click on E stores the pick W, each run at its own platform, figures to 1e-12). Run against the base
+  only for the related part (the old `route_of` panics on it); the others make lines to an anchor, which the base's
+  search cannot route.
+- **Parity** (scratch `trackroute/` in this session's scratchpad: `fp.py`, `new.ps1`, `base.ps1`, `fp/`), base
+  09c90ccd built here (identical to stage e's final fingerprints): game, game-left, mirror-right, mirror-left,
+  Amsterdam test, Vienna, real NYC, North America 383, t4018, three ways (old bytes; re-saved and loaded; the base's
+  re-saves loaded here) plus re-saved bytes: **36 of 36 identical** after step 1 (entry arrivals), step 2 (anchors in
+  the search, check, build_run), step 3 (the matchers) and on the final source.
+- **Vienna 12/600, confirmed.** With the side API's first-stop seeds read from the table (scratch experiment,
+  `vienna.ps1`, `vdiff.py`, removed): Vienna differs in 13 of 600 pairs (3a2 saw 12 on an older source), Amsterdam,
+  NYC and NA in none. All 13: a route before, none after, and no track brings trains in on the side the fingerprint
+  says the train arrived by (`arrived` is picked by index, `[None, Some(0), Some(1)][i % 3]`). So the difference is
+  the fingerprint's made-up arrival, never a real one; real hops now arrive on entries and read the table, and the
+  gates' line routes did not move.
+- **Timing** (agent profile, base / final, three rounds): Vienna 600 pairs 105-124 / 116-132 ms, reroute every line
+  1.7-2.3 / 1.9-2.5 ms, stop click 39-42 / 41-42 ms; NA pairs 6.1-7.5 / 7.2-8.9 ms; NYC pairs 1.3 / 1.5 ms; loads
+  within noise. By step, Vienna pairs: step 1 (entry arrivals) the base's, step 2 (anchors in the search) about 10%
+  slower; cause not found (marking the goal check inline changed nothing, and was taken out).
+- Cargo (`agent_cargo.py test`, final source): 788 + 1010 pass; `cargo check --features demand --all-targets` clean
+  (two old warnings), `agent_cargo.py wasm` and `npm run check` clean. checks/t3348-perdir.mjs passed (riders north
+  13,631, south 7,941, as 4a2). Smoke (Vite 6106, 4 cores, final WASM): 30 of 30, 219 s, the real save 109 lines and 13,248,663 rail trips a day (stage e's figure). Parity s6 on the final source: 36 of 36 identical.
+
+## Departures and things for later stages
+
+1. **No turn back at an anchor between a line's ends.** A split station lets a hop turn back there (at 600 s search
+   cost); an anchor does not, as a path of whole pieces cannot say a train turned inside a piece (`[P, P back]` reads
+   as a turn at the piece's end). A route that turns at such a stop takes the next way on instead, or has none. No
+   tool places anchors yet; **after g (T-4037) every new station is an anchor, so this would regress against
+   master** for lines reversing at a middle stop. For the orchestrator: either g's design gives a turn inside a piece
+   a path form (for example the turn entry pair plus the stop it falls on), or this is Anita's call.
+2. **A loop closing at an anchor** (a circle whose first stop is one) is not checked: `carry_on_side` knows anchors,
+   but `carry_worth` and `loop_kind` compare whole pieces. Filed with g.
+3. **`line_path` exports the whole end piece**, so the app's ribbon for a line ending at an anchor runs to the piece's
+   end (T-4037's app part, with the platform drawing e filed).
+4. **One-way legs as anchors** (her split with its leg stations off the track) are not routed as one-way
+   stations: `leg_heading` and `legs.rs` (one-way stations ordered by the track) still read ports and piece nodes, so
