 **Timetabled together.** When one slower line shares the stretch, the timetable sends each fast train out as late as it safely can, just behind a slow train. A fast line gains something only where it gains more than that gap. Two lines whose headways differ by less than {{sim/src/track/capacity.rs:FOLLOW_TIMED_MIN_S|0}} seconds count as one pattern. Each fast train has its own times: one that would catch the slow train ahead slows down from the station before, just enough to stay a safe distance behind it, and one that never gets close keeps its speed.
 
-**Quad track helps.** Quad track is two separate double tracks, so an express on its own pair keeps its speed.
+**Four tracks help.** Four tracks are two separate double tracks, so an express on its own pair keeps its speed.
 
 **Passing tracks.** A station with [[station-upgrades|passing tracks]] changes who waits. The slow train that stops there waits for the fast ones to pass, and the fast line keeps its speed. A slow train leaves {{sim/src/track/overtake.rs:PASS_CLEAR_S|0}} seconds after the last fast train has passed. It waits no longer than the gap between two fast trains, since the next one would catch it just the same, and never so long that it holds up its own next train. Several passing stations in a row pass trains the same way, as often as each line's headways make them meet. Each slow train has its own times, so it waits only for the fast trains that would catch it, and one that no fast train catches does not wait at all. Before the first passing station there is nowhere to step aside, so a fast train that catches a slow one on the way there follows it until the station. On weekends a line with its own weekend trains runs the average waits.
 
