@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+# Tracks
+
+**Single, double, four.** Single track is one track, and trains can run on it both ways or one way. Double track is two tracks {{sim/src/track/params.rs:TRACK_PITCH_M|0}} m apart, centre to centre, one for each way. Which one runs which way follows the side trains keep to: keeping right, the right-hand track carries trains away from you. Four tracks lie side by side, each with its own way. Zoomed out, double track is one thick line; zoomed in it opens into its two tracks.
+
+**Where track meets track.** Every track is drawn where it really is. Single track that carries on from one track of a double starts on that track and bends back to its own line over about {{app/src/map/network.ts:EASE_M|0}} m. The track it carries on from is the one you picked when you drew it. A flyover's one-way legs each start on the track their trains use.
+
+**Single-lead junctions.** Single track that runs both ways and joins one track of a double can still reach the other track: trains to and from it cross over the joined track. Zoomed in, that link shows as a short crossover drawn beside the junction. Trains crossing there get in each other's way, like at any flat junction ([[line-capacity]]).
+
+**Merges.** Single track drawn from the end of a double always carries on one of its tracks, never both. To bring the two tracks together into one, carry each on as its own single and draw one into the other: trains then leave on one and come back on the other. Double track from older games that meets single track with no track picked still narrows into it over {{app/src/map/network.ts:TAPER_M|0}} m, both tracks running into the single one.
+
+**One track at a time.** A click on track picks every track there; a second click picks the one track under the pointer, and the selection bar's small picture of the tracks picks any of them. One track of a double can be removed on its own: the rest becomes single track running that track's way. A line that needs the removed track takes another way, or the removal is refused naming the line. With one track picked, a double click follows that track to the junctions at both ends, through stations. One track of a double can also be reversed or made two way on its own, out to the junctions; lines move off it as they do for single track. Not where a station would end up with two tracks running the same way.
+
+**Setting a way.** A way set on single track goes along it to the junctions at both ends, so a one-way segment is never one way only part of the way. Lines that ran the other way take another way through their stops, or the change is refused naming the line.
+
+Exact rule: `docs/track-and-service.md` "Track settings" (its "Where tracks are drawn" and "Single track meeting double track") for where each track's ends sit, joins and the way single track runs; `docs/ui-map.md` "Widths and double track" for the drawing.
+In the code: `sim/src/track/drawn.rs` `drawn_tracks` `sim/src/track/compile.rs` `layout`, `compile_node` `sim/src/track/params.rs` `TRACK_PITCH_M` `app/src/map/network.ts` `trackPoints`, `leadStrokes`, `EASE_M`, `TAPER_M`
